title: What is recorded, and when
pid: 5a0e1097-4e09-4eba-9019-db922089e788
body: |2-
  Only the fork operation writes it, once, when it makes the clone, and
  only when the original's home skgrepo is not the clone's: a fork
  within one skgrepo has nothing to attribute. It records the note
  actually forked: forking a fork records the fork, and its skgrepo,
  not the note that fork came from. It is a record, not a view of the
  =forks= relation, so it outlives the original's deletion, a nodeMerge
  that acquires it, and its skgrepo not being loaded. The fields
  ('docs/api-and-formats.org', "The .skg file format"):
  - the original's pid;
  - its home skgrepo's name in the forker's config then: a local alias,
    so only the fallback;
  - the hash of that skgrepo's git remote ('origin', else the first),
    its URL normalised so that the ssh and https spellings of one
    repository agree: the skgrepo's identity, whatever anyone's config
    calls it. Hashed, so that a private remote's URL never appears in a
    public note. Left out when the skgrepo has no remote;
  - the HEAD commit of the gitrepo holding that skgrepo, so that what
    the original said can be read back; left out when it is no gitrepo;
  - =uncommitted: true= when the original's file differed from that
    commit: the commit then lacks the text forked, which only the
    clone's own first version holds (its first git commit, if the
    forker commits before editing).
